// GENERATED by scripts/build-courses.ts from src/lib/courses/content — edit those files, then run: npm run courses
import type { LessonText } from '../types';
const text: LessonText[][] = [[{"say":"Digital electronics starts with electricity. Let's learn how current flows through circuits.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Ohm's law: V = IR. Voltage (volts) pushes current (amperes) through resistance (ohms). Power P = IV."},{"t":"p","text":"In a series circuit, current is the same everywhere and resistances add. In a parallel circuit, voltage is the same across each branch and total resistance decreases."},{"t":"ex","problem":"A 9 V battery powers a 450 Ω resistor. Find the current.","steps":["I = V/R = 9/450"],"answer":"0.02 A (20 mA)"},{"t":"p","text":"Kirchhoff's laws: currents into a junction equal currents out, and voltages around any closed loop add to zero."}]},{"say":"Circuits are built from components with special jobs. Let's meet the most common ones.","blocks":[{"t":"list","items":["Resistors limit current. Their color bands show resistance values.","Capacitors store and release charge, smoothing voltage or creating time delays.","Diodes let current flow in only one direction. LEDs (light-emitting diodes) glow when current flows forward.","Transistors act as electronic switches or amplifiers. They're the building blocks of every computer chip."]},{"t":"key","text":"An LED needs a current-limiting resistor in series, or too much current will burn it out. R = (V_supply − V_LED) ÷ I_LED."},{"t":"ex","problem":"A 5 V supply drives an LED that drops 2 V and needs 20 mA. Find the resistor value.","steps":["R = (5 − 2) ÷ 0.020"],"answer":"150 Ω"}]}],[{"say":"Digital circuits work with two states, 0 and 1. Number systems let us represent anything with them.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Binary (base 2) uses 0 and 1. Hexadecimal (base 16) uses 0–9 and A–F, where each hex digit equals exactly 4 bits."},{"t":"ex","problem":"Convert binary 1010 1111 to hexadecimal.","steps":["Split into 4-bit groups: 1010 = A, 1111 = F"],"answer":"AF"},{"t":"p","text":"Converting decimal to binary: repeatedly divide by 2 and read the remainders bottom to top. 13 = 1101."},{"t":"p","text":"Digital signals are discrete (0 or 1), unlike analog signals, which vary continuously. Digital signals resist noise, which is why modern electronics use them."}]},{"say":"Logic gates are the building blocks of digital circuits. Each one makes a simple decision.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Basic gates:"},{"t":"list","items":["AND: output 1 only if all inputs are 1.","OR: output 1 if any input is 1.","NOT (inverter): flips the input.","NAND and NOR: AND and OR followed by NOT. They're universal gates: any circuit can be built from only NAND gates (or only NOR gates).","XOR: output 1 if the inputs are different."]},{"t":"p","text":"A truth table lists the output for every input combination. With n inputs, there are 2ⁿ rows."},{"t":"ex","problem":"A = 1, B = 0. What do AND, OR and XOR output?","steps":["AND: 0 (not both 1)","OR: 1 (at least one is 1)"],"answer":"XOR: 1 (inputs differ)"}]}],[{"say":"Boolean algebra lets us simplify logic circuits, so they use fewer gates and cost less.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"In Boolean algebra, AND is written as multiplication (AB), OR as addition (A + B), and NOT as an overbar or prime (A′)."},{"t":"p","text":"Useful laws:"},{"t":"list","items":["A + 0 = A, A · 1 = A, A + 1 = 1, A · 0 = 0.","A + A′ = 1, A · A′ = 0.","Distributive: A(B + C) = AB + AC.","De Morgan's theorems: (AB)′ = A′ + B′ and (A + B)′ = A′B′."]},{"t":"ex","problem":"Simplify AB + AB′.","steps":["Factor out A: A(B + B′)","B + B′ = 1"],"answer":"A"},{"t":"p","text":"Simplified expressions mean fewer gates, lower cost and faster circuits."}]},{"say":"Karnaugh maps give a visual way to simplify logic. Then we can turn a design problem into a working circuit.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"A Karnaugh map (K-map) arranges a truth table so adjacent cells differ by one variable. Group 1s in rectangles of 1, 2, 4 or 8 cells (powers of two), as large as possible. Each group becomes one simplified product term."},{"t":"p","text":"Design process: describe the problem in words → make a truth table → write the expression → simplify with a K-map or Boolean algebra → build the circuit with gates."},{"t":"ex","problem":"A safety system lights an alarm if a door is open (D) AND the system is armed (S). Write the logic.","steps":["Alarm = 1 only when both are 1"],"answer":"Alarm = DS (an AND gate)"},{"t":"p","text":"Common combinational circuits: adders (half and full adders add binary numbers), multiplexers (select one input) and decoders (activate one output based on a code)."}]}],[{"say":"Combinational circuits react instantly. Sequential circuits have memory, so the past affects the output.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"A flip-flop stores one bit. It changes state on a clock edge."},{"t":"list","items":["D flip-flop: the output Q takes the value of D at each clock edge.","JK flip-flop: J sets, K resets, and both 1 toggles the output.","T flip-flop: toggles when T = 1."]},{"t":"p","text":"A clock signal is a steady square wave that synchronizes changes. Counters are built from flip-flops: a 4-bit counter counts from 0 to 15 and then rolls over."},{"t":"p","text":"Registers store multiple bits; shift registers move bits left or right on each clock pulse."}]},{"say":"Microcontrollers are tiny computers that control everything from microwaves to robots.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"A microcontroller (like Arduino) combines a processor, memory and input/output pins on one chip. It runs a program that reads inputs (sensors, buttons) and controls outputs (LEDs, motors)."},{"t":"p","text":"Digital pins read or write HIGH (1) or LOW (0). Analog inputs read varying voltages through an analog-to-digital converter (ADC). Pulse-width modulation (PWM) simulates analog output by switching on and off rapidly, used to dim LEDs and control motor speed."},{"t":"code","text":"void loop() {\n  if (digitalRead(2) == HIGH) {\n    digitalWrite(13, HIGH);\n  } else {\n    digitalWrite(13, LOW);\n  }\n}"},{"t":"p","text":"Programmable logic devices (like FPGAs) let engineers build custom digital circuits in software."}]}]];
export default text;
